  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">WTLDS ALVARADO LAGOON SYS</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Veracruz coast is one of the most important waterfowl and shorebird continental migratory routes. However, extensive cattle grazing and forest fires threaten this wetland complex, especially its mangroves. Over the last 24 years approximately 12,000 acres of mangroves have been lost, and more than 56,000 wetland acres have been turned into grasslands. This project, with the help and involvement of locals, seeks to mitigate the destructive processes and to restore the wetland system to benefit habitat for the conservation of migratory birds The project will be implemented in the 715,175 acre Alvarado Wetland Complex (ALS). It provides habitat to at least 346 bird species, including Snowy Plover, Red Knot Green-winged Teal, American Golden Plover, Wilsons Plover, and Wilsons Phalarope. Phase 1B will restore 464.56 degraded wetland acres and enhance 2,767.68 additional wetland acres.</narrative>
  </description>
